# The pinned dnscrypt-proxy source, and the digest it has to carry.
#
# This file is a `sha256sum -c` file and is meant to be used as one, from the
# directory holding the archive:
#
#   sha256sum -c packaging/debian/dnscrypt-proxy.sha256     # from the repository root
#   (cd build && sha256sum -c ../packaging/debian/dnscrypt-proxy.sha256)
#
# That is why EVERY non-comment line below is a `<64 hex>  <file name>` line and
# nothing else. The first version of this file carried the tag's commit as a bare
# `tag-commit: <40 hex>` line, and `sha256sum -c` answered it with
#
#   sha256sum: WARNING: 1 line is improperly formatted
#
# on a file whose own comment told the reader to run exactly that command. The
# alternative -- dropping the instruction -- was rejected: the file is a checksum
# file, and telling a checksum file's reader not to use the checksum tool is worse
# than one extra comment. The commit therefore lives in a COMMENT, and the build
# script reads it back out by label.
#
# dnscrypt-proxy is not a dependency of this package: the binary is built from
# source into the package, so there is a source tree to pin. scripts/build-deb.sh
# verifies the line below against the archive it was given BEFORE it extracts
# anything, and it never builds a tree whose archive does not match, so a swapped
# archive fails the build rather than producing a resolver this project did not
# review.
#
# The archive is the upstream tag's tarball, unmodified:
#
#   curl -L -o build/dnscrypt-proxy-2.1.18.tar.gz \
#     https://github.com/DNSCrypt/dnscrypt-proxy/archive/refs/tags/2.1.18.tar.gz
#
# THE TAG'S COMMIT is recorded as well as the archive's digest, and it is not a
# checksum: it is `git ls-remote https://github.com/DNSCrypt/dnscrypt-proxy
# refs/tags/2.1.18` from the same fetch. A digest proves the archive is the same
# bytes every time and says nothing about whether those bytes are what the tag
# points at today, so with both recorded a future divergence is attributable to a
# RE-TAG -- which moves this SHA -- rather than to a transport change, which would
# not. A re-tag is a change somebody has to look at rather than one a pin silently
# absorbs. Neither number is a signature; see the task report's NOT VERIFIED list.
#
# tag-commit: 30c0a9e91c12a15c081ac90546dd657c4ab98fbc
9b810d862ba07c383cc0b8f9f7f1f2ca8f74a02f849d818c4c4d37cc21a7dfa6  dnscrypt-proxy-2.1.18.tar.gz
